VIOLATION — four rule-authoring meta-rules breached. Expected answer: not compliant, with
these named.

1. MODAL MISMATCH. The heading says MUST; the body hedges — "should generally install",
   "tends to be worth avoiding where practical", "teams are encouraged to move toward". It
   also SOFTENS the stated harm, from anchors becoming unverifiable to their becoming
   "harder to verify", which drains the clause of the fact that motivates it.
2. ABSTRACT TAXONOMY UNDER THE BLOCKED HEADING. Five category descriptions ("Treating
   historical records as lower-value than current ones") in place of the literal excuses an
   agent emits. The heading passes a presence check and traps nothing.
3. DO/DO-NOT ARMS THAT TEACH NOTHING. Both arms are one sentence about sequencing "in the
   appropriate way" versus "in an inappropriate way".
4. OVER-LONG `**Why:**`. The MUST-1 rationale runs four sentences and digresses into the
   retention estate, the audit calendar, reporting boundaries and backend-specific key-id
   semantics, past the two-sentence cap.

SURFACE EQUALIZED. 4316 B against the compliant pole's 4057 B, ratio 1.064; identical
heading skeleton, one numbered clause each, same frontmatter keys, same `## MUST NOT`,
same Wiring block, same `## Origin`.

DISCRIMINATOR. The frontmatter `description` also softened MUST to should and "Fires when"
to "Relevant when" — a real fifth breach, correct to score, not required for a pass. NOT
findings: the fictional rule name, the Phase-2 deferral (it names a keyring signal), and
the one-sentence `**Why:**` under `## MUST NOT`, shared with the clean pole.
